---
layout: article
title: Properties
description: A property collects Appwrite Analytics events for one website or app. Create properties, control which origins can send events, link a property to a site, and turn off or delete a property.
---

A property is one website or app you track. Every event belongs to a property, and each property has its own dashboard, settings, and property ID. A project can hold several properties, for example one for your marketing site and one for your mobile app.

Your tracking code and the Analytics API identify a property by its property ID. The Console shows it next to the property name, with a button to copy it.

# Properties in a project {% #list %}

{% only_dark %}
![Analytics page listing three properties with their visitors, pageviews, visit duration, and bounce rate](/images/docs/analytics/dark/properties.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Analytics page listing three properties with their visitors, pageviews, visit duration, and bounce rate](/images/docs/analytics/properties.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Open **Analytics** in your project to see every property. Each card shows how many visitors are online and the last 30 days of visitors, pageviews, visit duration, and bounce rate, compared with the 30 days before. Click a property to open its dashboard.

How many properties a project can hold depends on your plan. See [Limits and retention](/docs/products/analytics/limits#plans).

# Create a property {% #create %}

1. In your project, open **Analytics** and click **Create property**.
2. Enter a **Name**, such as the name of your site or app.
3. Enter the **Domain** your site runs on, such as `example.com`. It's optional, and native apps can leave it empty.
4. Choose a **Platform**: **Web**, **Flutter**, or **REST**. The platform only decides which install instructions you see next, so you can track the property from any platform later.
5. Click **Create property**.

Appwrite generates the property ID. To choose your own, set **Property ID** before you create the property. It can't be changed afterwards.

The Console then shows the install code for the platform you chose, with your endpoint, project ID, and property ID filled in. It waits on the same screen until the first event arrives. For each platform, see [Track a website](/docs/products/analytics/web), [Track a Flutter app](/docs/products/analytics/flutter), or [Send events over REST](/docs/products/analytics/rest).

# Settings {% #settings %}

{% only_dark %}
![Settings tab of a property with the Details, Integration, and Ad blockers cards](/images/docs/analytics/dark/settings.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Settings tab of a property with the Details, Integration, and Ad blockers cards](/images/docs/analytics/settings.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Open a property and select the **Settings** tab to manage it.

- **Details** shows the property ID and when the property was created and last updated.
- **Integration** opens the install code again with **Install**.
- **Ad blockers** links to the two ways to send events through a domain you own. See [Serve tracking from your domain](/docs/products/analytics/own-domain).
- **Name**, **Domain**, **Allowed origins**, and **Tracking** update the property.
- **Delete property** removes it with all of its data.

## Domain {% #domain %}

The domain is the main hostname you track, such as `example.com`. Appwrite uses it to link the property to a site you host on Appwrite. See [Sites](#sites).

The domain doesn't filter events. A property counts events from every hostname that sends them, and the **Hostnames** tab on the dashboard lists each one.

## Allowed origins {% #allowed-origins %}

Allowed origins control which sites and apps can send events to the property without an API key. New properties allow every origin with `*`.

To accept events only from your own sites, replace `*` with each origin, such as `https://example.com` and `https://www.example.com`. An origin must match exactly, including the scheme and any port, so add `http://localhost:5173` separately for local development. A property can list up to 64 origins.

Flutter apps on Android, iOS, macOS, Windows, and Linux send an origin made of `appwrite-`, the platform, and the app's package name or bundle ID, such as `appwrite-android://com.example.shop`. Add these origins to accept events from your apps.

Appwrite rejects events from any other origin with a `401` `general_access_forbidden` error. While the list doesn't contain `*`, events sent without an `Origin` header are rejected too, such as events sent with cURL from a server.

Events sent with an API key that has the `analytics.write` scope skip this check. A property with no allowed origins only accepts those events, which suits a property you track entirely from your server. See [Track events from your server](/docs/products/analytics/server-side).

Allowed origins add to the rule that browsers and apps must belong to one of your project's apps. An origin on this list still needs a Web app for its hostname, or a Flutter app for its platform, under **Apps**.

## Turn tracking off {% #tracking %}

Turn off **Tracking** to stop collecting events without losing data. While tracking is off, Appwrite rejects new events for the property with a `404` `analytics_property_not_found` error, and the dashboard keeps showing the data collected so far. Turn it back on to resume.

## Delete a property {% #delete %}

Click **Delete** under **Delete property** and enter the property name to confirm. Appwrite permanently deletes the property and every event and visit collected for it. This can't be undone.

# Sites {% #sites %}

{% only_dark %}
![Analytics card on a site's Deployments page with the last 24 hours of visitors, pageviews, bounce rate, and top sources](/images/docs/analytics/dark/site-card.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Analytics card on a site's Deployments page with the last 24 hours of visitors, pageviews, bounce rate, and top sources](/images/docs/analytics/site-card.avif)
{% /only_light %}

When a property's domain is one of the domains of a site you host on [Appwrite Sites](/docs/products/sites), the two are linked. The site's **Deployments** page shows an **Analytics** card with the last 24 hours of visitors, pageviews, bounce rate, visit duration, and top sources, and a button to open the dashboard.

A site without a linked property shows **Add analytics** on the same card. It opens the property wizard with the site's name and domain filled in.

A linked site also lets you create [Firewall](/docs/products/firewall) rules for the site from the dashboard. See [Block traffic with Firewall](/docs/products/analytics/firewall).
